WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Welcome everyone! Today, um, we're gonna show you how to set up the API for your integration.
This is like, super important because you need the API to basically communicate with our platform.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.000
Before you start, you'll need a few things. First, you need an active developer account.
You also need, uh, you need admin access to your organization.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.000
And, um, you're gonna need to know, I mean, you should definitely understand OAuth 2.0.
That's, you know, that's the security protocol we use.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
So step one is to navigate to the developer portal. You log in with your developer account.
Once you're in, you see a menu on the left side. Click on "Applications".
It's, uh, the first item in the menu basically.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:37.000
Okay so you'll see a button that says "Create New Application".
Click that and then, um, you fill out the form. You'll need to, like, enter your application name.
So for example, you could call it like "My Integration App" or something.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
After you create the app, the system generates two things: a client ID and a client secret.
These are, uh, these are really important. You gotta keep the secret safe, you know?
Don't share it. Like, literally never put it in your code or expose it anywhere.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.000
Next, you need to set up the redirect URI. That's where, um, where the user gets sent after authentication.
So you go back to your application settings and you paste in your callback URL.
Let's say your application is running on localhost:3000, you'd put http://localhost:3000/callback.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:59.000
Once you save that, you're basically ready to start using the API.
You use the client ID and secret to, like, request an access token.
That token is what lets you make API requests on behalf of your users.

00:00:59.500 --> 01:00:06.000
Oh and, um, one more thing I forgot to mention. You need to configure the scopes.
Scopes are basically permissions. So like, if you need to read user data, you request the "read:users" scope.
Or if you need to write data, you request "write:data". You combine them with spaces.
